<section id="theory" class="tab-content active">

<h2 class="section-title">
Complete Theory
</h2>


<div class="card definition">

<h2>1. What is Heat?</h2>

<p>
Heat is a form of energy transferred from one body to another
because of a difference in temperature.
</p>

<div class="formula">
<span class="formula-title">SI Unit of Heat</span>
Joule (J)
</div>

<div class="info">
Heat always flows spontaneously from a body at higher
temperature to a body at lower temperature.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>2. Heat vs Temperature</h2>

<div class="table-wrap">

<table>

<tr>
<th>Heat</th>
<th>Temperature</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Energy transferred because of temperature difference.</td>
<td>Measure of the thermal state of a body.</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>SI unit: Joule</td>
<td>SI unit: Kelvin</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Depends on amount of substance.</td>
<td>Does not directly depend on amount of substance.</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Measured using calorimetric methods.</td>
<td>Measured using a thermometer.</td>
</tr>

</table>

</div>

<div class="warning">
<strong>Exam Trap:</strong> Heat and temperature are not the same thing.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>3. Thermal Energy</h2>

<p>
The internal energy associated with the microscopic motion
and interactions of particles in a substance is related to
its thermal energy.
</p>

<p>
When a substance is heated, its internal energy may increase,
its temperature may rise, or it may undergo a change of state.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>4. Units of Heat</h2>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">

<h3>Joule</h3>

<p>
The SI unit of heat and energy.
</p>

<div class="formula">
1 J
</div>

</div>

<div class="mini">

<h3>Calorie</h3>

<p>
A traditional unit of heat.
</p>

<div class="formula">
1 cal ≈ 4.186 J
</div>

</div>

<div class="mini">

<h3>Kilocalorie</h3>

<p>
Commonly used as a larger unit of heat.
</p>

<div class="formula">
1 kcal = 1000 cal
</div>

</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>5. Specific Heat Capacity</h2>

<p>
Specific heat capacity is the amount of heat required to raise
the temperature of unit mass of a substance by one degree Celsius
(or one kelvin).
</p>

<div class="formula">
<span class="formula-title">Formula</span>
Q = mcΔT
</div>

<div class="formula">
<span class="formula-title">Specific Heat</span>
c = Q / (mΔT)
</div>

<div class="success">
A substance with high specific heat capacity requires more
heat to produce the same temperature rise in a given mass.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>6. Heat Capacity</h2>

<p>
Heat capacity is the amount of heat required to raise the
temperature of an entire body by one degree Celsius or one kelvin.
</p>

<div class="formula">
C = Q/ΔT
</div>

<div class="formula">
C = mc
</div>

<div class="info">
Specific heat capacity is a property per unit mass,
while heat capacity refers to the whole body.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>7. Thermal Expansion</h2>

<p>
Most substances expand when heated and contract when cooled.
The expansion may occur in length, area, or volume.
</p>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">

<h3>Linear Expansion</h3>

<div class="formula">
ΔL = αL₀ΔT
</div>

</div>

<div class="mini">

<h3>Area Expansion</h3>

<div class="formula">
ΔA = βA₀ΔT
</div>

</div>

<div class="mini">

<h3>Volume Expansion</h3>

<div class="formula">
ΔV = γV₀ΔT
</div>

</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>8. Change of State</h2>

<p>
A substance can change from one state to another when heat
is supplied or removed.
</p>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">
<h3>Melting</h3>
<p>
Solid → Liquid
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Freezing</h3>
<p>
Liquid → Solid
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Vaporisation</h3>
<p>
Liquid → Gas
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Condensation</h3>
<p>
Gas → Liquid
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Sublimation</h3>
<p>
Solid → Gas directly
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Deposition</h3>
<p>
Gas → Solid directly
</p>
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>9. Latent Heat</h2>

<p>
Latent heat is the heat absorbed or released during a change
of state without a change in temperature.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Q = mL
</div>

<p>
where L is the specific latent heat.
</p>

<div class="warning">
During a pure phase change at constant pressure, the supplied
heat goes into changing the state rather than raising temperature.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>10. Melting Point</h2>

<p>
The melting point is the temperature at which a substance
changes from solid to liquid under a specified pressure.
</p>

<div class="info">
At the melting point, solid and liquid phases can coexist
in equilibrium.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>11. Boiling Point</h2>

<p>
The boiling point is the temperature at which the vapour
pressure of a liquid equals the surrounding pressure.
</p>

<div class="formula">
At boiling point:
Vapour pressure = External pressure
</div>

<div class="success">
Boiling point generally decreases when external pressure decreases.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>12. Evaporation</h2>

<p>
Evaporation is the slow conversion of liquid into vapour
from the surface of the liquid.
</p>

<h3>Factors Affecting Evaporation</h3>

<ul>
<li>Higher temperature → faster evaporation.</li>
<li>Larger surface area → faster evaporation.</li>
<li>Higher wind speed → faster evaporation.</li>
<li>Lower humidity → faster evaporation.</li>
</ul>

<div class="success">
Evaporation produces cooling because higher-energy molecules
preferentially escape from the liquid surface.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>13. Why Does Sweating Cool the Body?</h2>

<p>
Sweat evaporates from the surface of the skin. Evaporation
requires energy, which is taken from the body, causing cooling.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Evaporation → Heat absorbed → Cooling
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>14. Anomalous Expansion of Water</h2>

<p>
Water behaves differently from many other substances between
0°C and 4°C.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Water has maximum density at 4°C
</div>

<p>
When water is cooled from 4°C to 0°C, it expands instead of
contracting.
</p>

<div class="success">
This unusual property helps aquatic life survive in cold regions
because ice floats and the water below can remain liquid.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>15. Thermal Equilibrium</h2>

<p>
When two bodies in thermal contact reach the same temperature,
they are said to be in thermal equilibrium.
</p>

<div class="formula">
T₁ = T₂
</div>

<div class="info">
At thermal equilibrium, there is no net heat flow between
the bodies.
</div>

</div>

</section>


