<!-- ================================================= -->

<section id="temperature" class="tab-content">

<h2 class="section-title">
Temperature & Temperature Scales
</h2>


<div class="card">

<h2>1. Celsius Scale</h2>

<p>
The Celsius scale uses 0°C as the freezing point of water
and 100°C as the boiling point of water at standard atmospheric
pressure.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Freezing point = 0°C
</div>

<div class="formula">
Boiling point = 100°C
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>2. Kelvin Scale</h2>

<p>
Kelvin is the SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature.
</p>

<div class="formula">
K = °C + 273.15
</div>

<div class="formula">
°C = K − 273.15
</div>

<div class="warning">
Kelvin temperatures are written without the degree symbol:
<strong>300 K</strong>, not 300°K.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>3. Fahrenheit Scale</h2>

<div class="formula">
Freezing point of water = 32°F
</div>

<div class="formula">
Boiling point of water = 212°F
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>4. Temperature Conversion</h2>

<div class="formula">
°C/5 = (°F−32)/9
</div>

<div class="formula">
°C = 5(°F−32)/9
</div>

<div class="formula">
°F = 9°C/5 + 32
</div>

<div class="formula">
K = °C + 273.15
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>5. Important Temperature Values</h2>

<div class="table-wrap">

<table>

<tr>
<th>Condition</th>
<th>Celsius</th>
<th>Kelvin</th>
<th>Fahrenheit</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Freezing point of water</td>
<td>0°C</td>
<td>273.15 K</td>
<td>32°F</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Boiling point of water</td>
<td>100°C</td>
<td>373.15 K</td>
<td>212°F</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Absolute zero</td>
<td>−273.15°C</td>
<td>0 K</td>
<td>−459.67°F</td>
</tr>

</table>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>6. Absolute Zero</h2>

<p>
Absolute zero is the theoretical lowest possible temperature.
It corresponds to 0 K.
</p>

<div class="formula">
0 K = −273.15°C
</div>

<div class="info">
At temperatures approaching absolute zero, thermal motion is
reduced to extremely low levels.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>7. Thermometers</h2>

<p>
A thermometer measures temperature by using a physical property
that changes predictably with temperature.
</p>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">
<h3>Clinical Thermometer</h3>
<p>
Used for measuring human body temperature.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Laboratory Thermometer</h3>
<p>
Used to measure temperatures in laboratory experiments.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Digital Thermometer</h3>
<p>
Uses electronic sensors and displays the temperature digitally.
</p>
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>8. Thermal Expansion in Daily Life</h2>

<div class="accordion">

<button onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
Why are gaps left between railway tracks?
</button>

<div class="accordion-content">

<p>
Metal expands when heated. Gaps provide room for thermal
expansion and help reduce the risk of buckling.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="accordion">

<button onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
Why are electric wires not stretched tightly?
</button>

<div class="accordion-content">

<p>
Wires contract in cold weather and expand in hot weather.
Some slack allows for these changes in length.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="accordion">

<button onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
Why does a metal lid loosen when heated?
</button>

<div class="accordion-content">

<p>
Heating causes the metal lid to expand, making it easier
to open.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="accordion">

<button onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
Why are expansion joints used in bridges?
</button>

<div class="accordion-content">

<p>
They allow bridge materials to expand and contract due to
temperature changes without producing excessive stress.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</div>

