<section id="transfer" class="tab-content">

<h2 class="section-title">
Modes of Heat Transfer
</h2>


<div class="card">

<h2>1. Conduction</h2>

<p>
Conduction is the transfer of heat through a substance without
bulk movement of the substance as a whole.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Heat transfer through solid → Conduction
</div>

<div class="success">
Metals are generally good conductors of heat.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>2. Convection</h2>

<p>
Convection is heat transfer through the bulk movement of
a fluid such as a liquid or gas.
</p>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">

<h3>Natural Convection</h3>

<p>
Occurs because density differences create fluid motion.
</p>

</div>

<div class="mini">

<h3>Forced Convection</h3>

<p>
Fluid motion is produced using fans, pumps or other devices.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>3. Radiation</h2>

<p>
Radiation is heat transfer by electromagnetic waves and does
not require a material medium.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Radiation → No medium required
</div>

<div class="success">
Heat from the Sun reaches Earth mainly by radiation.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>4. Conduction vs Convection vs Radiation</h2>

<div class="table-wrap">

<table>

<tr>
<th>Mode</th>
<th>Medium</th>
<th>Main Mechanism</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Conduction</td>
<td>Usually solids</td>
<td>Particle-to-particle energy transfer</td>
<td>Heating a metal rod</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Convection</td>
