<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 1 — Heat Required
</div>

<p>
Find the heat required to raise the temperature of 2 kg
of a substance having specific heat capacity 500 J/kg·K
by 10 K.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Q = mcΔT
</div>

<div class="formula">
Q = 2 × 500 × 10
</div>

<div class="formula">
Q = 10,000 J
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>10,000 J</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 2 — Find Specific Heat
</div>

<p>
5000 J of heat raises the temperature of 2 kg of a material
by 5 K. Find its specific heat capacity.
</p>

<div class="formula">
c = Q/(mΔT)
</div>

<div class="formula">
c = 5000/(2×5)
</div>

<div class="formula">
c = 500 J/kg·K
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>500 J/kg·K</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 3 — Latent Heat
</div>

<p>
How much heat is required to melt 2 kg of a substance if
its specific latent heat of fusion is 200,000 J/kg?
</p>

<div class="formula">
Q = mL
</div>

<div class="formula">
Q = 2 × 200000
</div>

<div class="formula">
Q = 400000 J
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>4 × 10⁵ J</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 4 — Celsius to Fahrenheit
</div>

<p>
Convert 40°C into Fahrenheit.
</p>

<div class="formula">
°F = 9°C/5 + 32
</div>

<div class="formula">
°F = (9×40)/5 + 32
</div>

<div class="formula">
°F = 72 + 32 = 104°F
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>104°F</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 5 — Celsius to Kelvin
</div>

<p>
Convert 27°C into Kelvin.
</p>

<div class="formula">
K = °C + 273.15
</div>

<div class="formula">
K = 27 + 273.15
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>300.15 K</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 6 — Heat Capacity
</div>

<p>
A body requires 3000 J of heat for a temperature rise of
15 K. Find its heat capacity.
</p>

<div class="formula">
C = Q/ΔT
</div>

<div class="formula">
C = 3000/15 = 200 J/K
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>200 J/K</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 7 — Linear Expansion
</div>

<p>
A metal rod has initial length 2 m. Its coefficient of linear
expansion is 1×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹ and its temperature increases by 100 K.
Find the increase in length.
</p>

<div class="formula">
ΔL = αL₀ΔT
</div>

<div class="formula">
ΔL = 1×10⁻⁵ × 2 × 100
</div>

<div class="formula">
ΔL = 0.002 m
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>0.002 m = 2 mm</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 8 — Calorimetry
</div>

<p>
A hot object loses 5000 J of heat in an insulated container.
How much heat is gained by the surroundings in the ideal
calorimetry model?
</p>

<div class="formula">
Heat Lost = Heat Gained
</div>

<div class="formula">
Q<sub>gained</sub> = 5000 J
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>5000 J</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 9 — Temperature Difference
</div>

<p>
The temperature of an object rises from 20°C to 70°C.
Find the temperature change.
</p>

<div class="formula">
ΔT = 70 − 20
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>50°C = 50 K temperature difference</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 10 — Evaporation
</div>

<p>
Why does water evaporate faster when spread over a larger
surface area?
</p>

<div class="formula">
Larger Surface Area → More Molecules at Surface
→ Faster Evaporation
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>Evaporation increases with surface area.</strong>
</div>

</div>

</section>


